          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Anxiety sits in the mental and behavioural chapter. Where would a physical symptom of it, like palpitations, be classified?",
          "answer": "Palpitations are a symptom, so they are usually classified in Chapter 18 of ICD-10-CM, the chapter for symptoms, signs and abnormal findings (R00 to R99). The circulatory chapter document says that palpitations, along with chest pain, stay in Chapter 18 \"until a diagnosis is recorded\" (coding-icd10-chapter-09-circulatory). The symptom chapter document adds that an R code is used when a symptom is recorded and no more specific diagnosis has been established (coding-icd10-chapter-18-symptoms).\n\nAnxiety disorders are in F40 to F48. That range includes \"the somatic symptom categories,\" which is the closest link to physical symptoms (coding-icd10-chapter-05-mental). The retrieved passages do not say which code a palpitation would get when it is linked to anxiety, or whether a clinician would record anxiety as the diagnosis in that case. Those decisions depend on the clinical record and the coding rules, so the tools could not answer them.\n\nPalpitations can have many causes, and only a clinician who has examined the person can work out what is behind them. If palpitations are new, frequent, or come with chest pain, fainting, or shortness of breath, seek medical care promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
